body:
I think this will lead to a memory leak, but I'm not 100% sure.

The reason I suspect it is that I don't think the destructors for each `Variant` in `args` will run automatically. This is fine for any variant type where all the data is part of the `Variant` class (because this uses `alloca()` to put their data on the stack), but some types refer to memory elsewhere (for example: `String`s, `RefCounted` object pointers or any of the `Packed*Array` types) and use reference counting, which won't be decremented when this function exits, leaving the ref count artificially inflated by one.

So, I think we need to add some code at the end that manually calls `~Variant()`, like:

```suggestion
	Variant *args = (Variant *)alloca(sizeof(Variant) * count);
	const Variant **argptrs = (const Variant **)alloca(sizeof(Variant *) * count);

	for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
		jobject jobj = p_env->GetObjectArrayElement(p_parameters, i);
		ERR_FAIL_NULL_V(jobj, Callable());
		memnew_placement(&args[i], Variant(_jobject_to_variant(p_env, jobj)));
		argptrs[i] = &args[i];
		p_env->DeleteLocalRef(jobj);
	}

	Callable ret = Callable(obj, str_method).bindp(argptrs, count);
	
	for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
		args[i].~Variant();
	}
	
	return ret;
```

I don't think this should cause any problems, because `.bindp()` will copy each `Variant`, which will increment the ref count (if there is a refcount for that particular variant type).

